    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1065">

                <pb facs="1308" n="1290" sameAs="frus:frus1946v11/pg1290"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">833.61311/4–1246</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Uruguay</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dawson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Montevideo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-04-12T11:00:00-03:00">April 12, 1946—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 11:17 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>231. Following is summary of views expressed to me by Uruguayan close to
                        President<anchor n="53" xml:id="d1065-f1"/> who has spent week in Buenos
                    Aires in effort to obtain wheat.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“Argentine could furnish Uruguay 50,000 tons requested. Refusal due solely to
                            Perón<anchor n="54" xml:id="d1065-f2"/> who is using wheat as instrument
                        in building up anti-US bloc having promised 50,000 tons each to Bolivia and
                        Paraguay and 100,000 to Peru. Perón resents attitude Amezaga administration
                        and is grateful to Herreristas. He aims to bring Uruguay under control and
                        encourage establishment government subservient to him. For present his
                        policy will be to humiliate, embarrass and discredit Amezaga administration
                        and build up and aid Herreristas with view to victory in November elections.
                        Attitude towards Uruguay already verges on aggression and constitutes
                        serious danger for Uruguay and US. Reduction Uruguay to vassal state would
                        be great victory for Perón and defeat for US. Informant convinced US must
                        and will assist Uruguay insofar as practicable”.<anchor n="55"
                            xml:id="d1065-f3"/></p>
                </quote>
                <p>Full memo of conversation<anchor n="56" xml:id="d1065-f4"/> by courier. Repeated
                    Buenos Aires.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dawson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1065-f1" n="53" xml:id="d1065-f1-content">
                <p>Juan José Amezaga.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1065-f2" n="54" xml:id="d1065-f2-content">
                <p>Juan Perón, President elect of Argentina.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1065-f3" n="55" xml:id="d1065-f3-content">
                <p>For the opinion of the Ambassador in Argentina on the danger of a southern bloc,
                    see despatch 583, August 15, 1946, <ref target="#pg297">p. 297</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1065-f4" n="56" xml:id="d1065-f4-content">
